SOCKET(2)                      P32W Unix                       SOCKET(2)

NAME
       socket, connect, bind, listen, accept - Internet sockets

SYNOPSIS
       #include <sys/socket.h>
       #include <netinet/in.h>

       int socket(int domain, int type, int protocol);
       int connect(int fd, const struct sockaddr *addr, socklen_t len);
       int bind(int fd, const struct sockaddr *addr, socklen_t len);
       int listen(int fd, int backlog);
       int accept(int fd, struct sockaddr *addr, socklen_t *len);

DESCRIPTION
       A socket is one end of a network connection, and a file
       descriptor: read(2), write(2), close(2), dup(2) and select(2)
       work on it, and it is passed on by fork(2) and exec(2) like
       any other. They are BSD's sockets, for the Internet (IPv4)
       only. The TCP/IP itself is done by the network coprocessor
       on the I/O card (a Pico 2 W with a W5500), which has 8
       sockets for the whole system.

       socket makes one: domain is AF_INET; type is SOCK_STREAM
       (TCP, a reliable stream of bytes) or SOCK_DGRAM (UDP,
       separate datagrams); protocol is 0, or IPPROTO_TCP or
       IPPROTO_UDP to match. SOCK_STREAM with IPPROTO_TLS is a TCP
       connection encrypted with TLS by the coprocessor, which also
       checks the server's certificate: give the server's name
       first, with setsockopt(fd, IPPROTO_TLS, TLS_HOSTNAME, name,
       strlen(name)). Then reading and writing it is like any TCP
       socket, so a program can fetch https:// pages.

       Addresses are struct sockaddr_in: sin_family AF_INET, then
       sin_port and sin_addr.s_addr in network byte order (htons,
       htonl, inet_addr: see inet(3)).

       connect on a stream socket makes the connection, waiting for
       it. With O_NONBLOCK set (fcntl(2)) it returns at once with
       EINPROGRESS; select(2) then says the socket is ready to write
       when the connection is made, and getsockopt SO_ERROR gives
       the outcome. On a datagram socket connect only records the
       address that send(2) and write(2) send to.

       bind gives a socket the port it is reached at (the address
       is ignored: the coprocessor has one). Ports below 1024 are
       for root. listen makes a bound stream socket wait for
       connections; accept takes the next, waiting for one unless
       O_NONBLOCK is set, and returns a new descriptor for it,
       filling addr with the peer's address if addr is not null.
       select(2) reports a listening socket ready to read when a
       connection is waiting.

RETURN VALUE
       socket and accept return a descriptor; the others 0. -1 on
       an error, with errno set.

ERRORS
       ENETDOWN        No coprocessor, or it has stopped answering.
       ENOBUFS         All 8 of the coprocessor's sockets are in use.
       EAFNOSUPPORT    domain is not AF_INET.
       EPROTONOSUPPORT The protocol does not go with the type
                       (TLS over UDP).
       ENOTSOCK        fd is not a socket.
       ECONNREFUSED    Nothing listens at that address and port.
       ETIMEDOUT       No answer.
       EHOSTUNREACH    No route to the host.
       ECONNABORTED    The TLS handshake failed, or the server's
                       certificate is not trusted or not for its name.
       EINPROGRESS     O_NONBLOCK: the connection is being made.
       EISCONN         Already connected.
       EADDRINUSE      The port is in use.
       EACCES          A port below 1024, and not root.
       EAGAIN          O_NONBLOCK, and no connection is waiting.
       EINTR           A signal came while waiting.

SEE ALSO
       send(2), getsockopt(2), select(2), fcntl(2), netctl(2),
       inet(3), docs/net-mailbox.md

BUGS
       IPv4 only. 8 sockets in all, and a listening socket is one
       of them. A host's own address cannot be connected to (the
       W5500 has no loopback). In the emulators (p32emu -N), the
       Mac is 10.0.2.2 and P32W's ports below 1024 are the Mac's
       8000 higher.
